    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0112",
      "sequence": 124,
      "meaning": "Without knowing Rāma, even falsehood appears true, just as a rope seems a serpent until recognized; knowing him makes the world disappear as dream-delusion vanishes on waking. Śiva bows to that same Rāma in child form, whose name makes every accomplishment accessible, and invokes the courtyard-playing child of Daśaratha as abode of auspiciousness and remover of misfortune. Bowing to Rāma, Tripurāri joyfully speaks nectar-like words: blessed is the mountain’s daughter, for no one equals her as benefactor. By asking about Raghupati’s story—the Ganges that purifies every world—she has acted for the world’s good from love for Raghubīra’s feet. By Rāma’s grace, Śiva says, not even in dreams does grief, delusion, doubt or error exist in her mind.",
