    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0113",
      "sequence": 125,
      "meaning": "Yet her voiced uncertainty benefits everyone who tells and hears it. Śiva uses forceful devotional metaphors: ears that never hear Hari’s story are like snake-holes; eyes that never behold saints are merely painted peacock-feather eyes; a head that never bows at Hari’s and the teacher’s feet is like a bitter gourd; a living person whose heart lacks devotion to Hari resembles a corpse; a tongue that does not sing Rāma’s virtues is a frog’s tongue; and a breast that does not thrill on hearing Hari’s deeds is hard and pitiless as a thunderbolt. He asks Girijā to hear Rāma’s play, performed for the gods’ welfare and capable of bewildering demons. Rāma’s story is like the wish-granting cow, giving every happiness to those who serve it; knowing an assembly of saints to be heaven itself, who would not listen?",
