    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0114",
      "sequence": 126,
      "meaning": "Rāma’s beautiful story is a clapping of hands that drives away the birds of doubt and an axe for Kali’s tree; the mountain’s daughter should listen reverently. The Vedas sing Rāma’s countless names, virtues, deeds, births and acts. As Bhagavān Rāma is infinite, so too are his stories, fame and manifold virtues. Even so, seeing Umā’s deep love, Śiva will tell according to what he has heard and his own understanding. Her naturally lovely, joy-giving and saint-approved question pleases him, but one statement—made under delusion—does not: her suggestion that the Rāma praised by Vedas and contemplated by sages might be someone else. Only degraded people possessed by delusion’s ghost speak or hear this; turned from Hari’s feet, such heretics cannot distinguish false from true.",
