    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0115",
      "sequence": 127,
      "meaning": "Śiva describes such speakers as ignorant, unlearned, blind and unfortunate, attached to the grime of sensual objects; wanton, deceitful and especially crooked, they have never seen a saints’ assembly even in dreams. Without regard for gain or loss, they utter speech rejected by the Vedas. How can the wretched see Rāma’s form when their inner mirror is dirty and they lack eyes? Unable to discern qualified from qualityless reality, they babble many inventions. Wandering under Hari’s māyā, nothing incoherent is beyond them. Like mad people, spirit-possessed and intoxicated, they do not speak considered words. One should not heed those who have drunk the liquor of great delusion and pride. Reflecting thus, Girijā should abandon doubt and worship Rāma’s feet; Śiva’s words are sunbeams dispelling confusion’s darkness.",
